UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"result","module":2,"title":"Severe cutaneous reactions: SJS/TEN, DRESS, bullous","body":"**PRESENTATION AND RED FLAGS**\n• ASCO severe cutaneous reactions (SJS, TEN, DRESS) can cause fever, widespread rash, skin pain, skin sloughing, facial or upper-extremity edema, pustules. Ask about skin pain like sunburn, fever, malaise, eye discomfort, mouth or nose sores, trouble swallowing, genital or perianal sores. [1]\n• Exam: full skin and ALL mucous membranes (eyes, nose, mouth, genitals, perianal); lymphadenopathy and facial or distal-limb swelling suggest DRESS. [1]\n• ASCO: limited body-surface involvement with bullae or erosions carries high concern for progression to grade 3 or 4. [1]\n• SITC: SJS/TEN is rare and life-threatening with mucosal involvement; it can be rapid or progress from a lichenoid eruption that fails usual treatment. [3]\n• ESMO: rare reports of lethal reactions (TEN, severe SJS, DRESS); severe reactions need immediate interruption of the inhibitor, specialist treatment and monitoring. [2]\n\n**WORKUP**\n• ASCO: CBC with differential, liver and kidney function; urinalysis in DRESS (nephritis); blood cultures if febrile; skin biopsy (full-thickness epidermal necrosis in SJS/TEN; autoimmune blistering, AGEP, paraneoplastic pemphigus); serial photography; consider early burn-center admission if mucous-membrane involvement or blistering. Bullous: exclude infection or another drug; dermatology biopsy with direct immunofluorescence. [1]\n• SITC: for blisters or mucosal involvement suspect lichenoid rash, pemphigus, pemphigoid or SJS/TEN; biopsy with direct immunofluorescence and antibody tests; CBC, CMP, body-surface-area assessment. [3]\n\n**GRADING (ASCO)**\n• Severe cutaneous reactions: no grade 1-2. Grade 3: skin sloughing under 10% of body surface with signs of mucosal involvement (erythema, purpura, epidermal detachment, mucous-membrane detachment). Grade 4: erythema and blistering or sloughing of 10% or more with associated signs and/or systemic symptoms, and concerning blood work abnormalities (e.g. raised liver tests in DRESS/DIHS).\n• Bullous dermatoses: grade 1 under 10% body surface without erythema; grade 2 blisters 10-30%; grade 3 sloughing over 30% with pain and limited self-care; grade 4 blisters over 30% with fluid or electrolyte abnormalities. [1]\n\n**TREATMENT**\n• **Suspected SJS or any mucous-membrane involvement, not isolated stomatitis (ASCO):** stop the inhibitor, dermatology, monitor closely at any grade.\n• **SJS/TEN/DRESS grade 3 (ASCO):** hold; dermatology; admit to a burn unit and/or wound services (fluid and electrolytes, minimise water loss, prevent infection); emollients or petrolatum, oral antihistamines, high-strength topical corticosteroids; IV methylprednisolone (or equivalent) 0.5-1 mg/kg converted to oral on response, weaning over at least 4 weeks; ophthalmology, ENT, urology, gynecology for mucosal disease.\n• **SJS/TEN/DRESS grade 4 (ASCO):** permanently discontinue; admit to a burn unit or ICU with dermatology and wound care; IV methylprednisolone 1-2 mg/kg, tapering when toxicity resolves; IVIG or cyclosporine may be considered if severe or steroid-unresponsive; consider pain or palliative input, and admission in DRESS.\n• **Bullous, ASCO:** grade 2 hold, dermatology, petrolatum and bandages over erosions, high-potency topical steroid reassessed every 3 days, low threshold for prednisone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day tapered over at least 4 weeks; grade 3 hold, consider admission, IV methylprednisolone 1-2 mg/kg then oral, taper at least 4 weeks (consider IVIG or rituximab if bullous pemphigoid); grade 4 permanently discontinue, admit under dermatology, IV methylprednisolone 1-2 mg/kg. [1]\n• SITC gives no systemic steroid dose for SJS/TEN or DRESS, and ESMO places SJS/TEN, DRESS and bullous management in its supplement, which is not shown here. SITC: interrupt the inhibitor for blisters or mucosal involvement; grade 2 or higher skin events can recur after the taper, so dermatology or a steroid-sparing agent is recommended. [3][2]\n\n**DO NOT**\n• ASCO: the usual prohibition of corticosteroids in SJS does not apply here, because the mechanism is T-cell immune-directed (stated in ASCO''s grade 3 row). Do not withhold steroids on that rule. [1]\n• Do not treat blistering or mucosal skin involvement as a routine rash; ESMO says severe reactions need immediate interruption of the inhibitor. [1][2]\n\n**DISPOSITION AND CHECKPOINT INHIBITOR**\n• ASCO: consider early burn-center admission if mucous-membrane involvement or blistering; burn unit and/or wound services at SJS/TEN/DRESS grade 3; burn unit or ICU at SJS/TEN/DRESS grade 4. [1]\n• **Clinical note (myMedKitt editor, not from the irAE guidelines):** disposition is decided with the treating oncologist.\n• ASCO, SJS/TEN/DRESS: hold the inhibitor at grade 3; permanently discontinue at grade 4. [1]\n• ASCO, suspected SJS or any mucous-membrane involvement (not isolated stomatitis), regardless of grade: discontinue the inhibitor and consult dermatology. [1]\n• Labels: suspected SJS, TEN or DRESS withhold; confirmed permanently discontinue (see the drug card). Individual drug labels may differ from the guidelines (see the drug''s card). [4]\n\nNavigation: [DRESS Syndrome consult](#/tree/dress-syndrome).","citation":[1,2,3,4],"recommendation":"**Suspected SJS or any mucosal involvement: stop the inhibitor, dermatology, consider early burn-center admission; IV methylprednisolone by grade (ASCO); at SJS/TEN/DRESS grade 4, IVIG or cyclosporine may be considered if severe or steroid-unresponsive. Editor note: disposition is decided with the treating oncologist.**","confidence":"recommended","summary":"SJS/TEN/DRESS G3 hold + IV methylpred 0.5-1 mg/kg; G4 stop ICI + burn unit or ICU + 1-2 mg/kg; bullous G3-4 IV methylpred 1-2 mg/kg (ASCO)","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'odse-tox-severe-skin' AND tree_id = 'onc-drug-side-effects';

